     Each _a_r_g_u_m_e_n_t is one token of an expression; separate com-
     mand-line arguments correspond to separate tokens, so
     operators must stand as their own argument, surrounded by
     spaces.  eexxpprr evaluates the expression and writes the
     result, followed by a newline, to the standard output.  It
     exits 0 if the resulting value is true (a non-null string
     other than 00), 1 if it is false, and 2 on a syntax error or
     overflow.

     An _a_r_g_u_m_e_n_t that looks like an optionally signed decimal in-
     teger is treated as a number wherever the operator requires
     one; otherwise it is compared or concatenated as a string.
     Integers are held as signed long values; no check is made
     for overflow in the arithmetic operators.

     Operators, in increasing order of precedence (operators
     listed together bind equally):

     { _e_1 , _e_2 , _e_3 }
           Return _e_2 if _e_1 is true, otherwise _e_3. If the third
           argument is omitted, it defaults to 00.

     _e_1 | _e_2
           Return _e_1 if it is true, otherwise _e_2.

     _e_1 & _e_2
